    {
      "title": "SpaceX's Starship launches into orbit for first time but flight ends early",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/world-news/spacex-s-starship-launches-into-orbit-for-first-time-but-flight-ends-early-126092900052_1.html",
      "description": "SpaceX launched its enormous Starship into orbit for the first time Monday and successfully delivered the most advanced Starlink satellites yet, but cut the flight short to ensure safety.\nThe spacecraft reentered over the Pacific and splashed down north of Hawaii three hours after blasting off from Texas. The company had been aiming for a 10-hour flight, spanning six full laps around Earth, to prove its readiness for NASA's Artemis moon program.\nStarship tipped over and erupted in flames upon splashdown, a dramatic end to the mission.\nFounder Elon Musk's Starship almost didn't make it to orbit when one of its engines shut down prematurely. But with everything else working well and the bad engine no longer needed, flight controllers decided, after several tense minutes, to proceed as planned.\n\"Starship is orbital,\" Mission Control announced to cheers.\nNASA Administrator Jared Isaacman congratulated SpaceX on reaching orbit and \"managing every step in a safe, responsible and especia",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-09-29T02:52:52"
    },
    {
      "title": "SpaceX's Starship goes orbital, deploying first next-gen Starlinks",
